<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alaeddin Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–13th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Alâeddin Camii</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alaeddin Mosque is the main mosque of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, standing on Alaeddin Hill at the heart of the city. Begun in the twelfth century and completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> around 1220, it has a large hall of reused antique columns and a courtyard containing the tombs of several Seljuk sultans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque.jpg" alt="Alaeddin Mosque" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Mathis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alaedin_Camii.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
